                <head>363. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On May 17, 1965, President Pak met with President Johnson, who congratulated him on the progress of Korean
                    negotiations with Japan (see <ref target="#d48">Document 48</ref>). President
                    Pak stated that negotiations could be completed in June and, despite internal
                    opposition, believed an agreement would be concluded. President Pak met with
                    Secretary Rusk on May 18 and briefly
                    discussed the Korea-Japan negotiations. Secretary Rusk urged an early conclusion to the talks, but President Pak
                    raised the issue of Tokto Island (Takeshima), to which both Korea and Japan laid
                    claim, characterizing it as an intractable problem between the two countries.
                    Secretary Rusk suggested erecting a
                    jointly operated lighthouse, allowing both sides a share of the island and
                    gradually diffusing the issue. President Pak commented that such a project “just
                    would not work.” (See <ref target="#d50">Document 50</ref>.)</p>
            </div>
